
# Datasets & Dataloaders

## Why bother?

So far we have been handing `list(X_train_t, y_train_t)` straight to `fit()`. That works when the data is:

* already in memory as tensors,

* small enough that shuffling and batching the full tensor is cheap, and

* shaped as a simple `(x, y)` pair that luz can split into batches.

For anything more interesting - images loaded lazily from disk, on-the-fly augmentation, text, or datasets that don't fit in RAM - we reach for the torch `dataset()` / `dataloader()` abstraction.


## `tensor_dataset()`

The simplest dataset is a thin wrapper around a set of tensors - `.getitem(i)` returns the `i`-th row of each:

::: {.xsmall}
```{r}
train_ds = tensor_dataset(X_train_t, y_train_t)

length(train_ds)
```
:::

. . .

::: {.xsmall}
```{r}
ex = train_ds$.getitem(1)
str(ex, max.level = 1)
ex[[1]]$shape; ex[[2]]$item()
```
:::

::: {.aside}
`tensor_dataset()` is the R analogue of PyTorch's `TensorDataset`. Any tensors you pass get indexed along their first dimension.
:::


## `dataloader()`

A dataloader wraps a dataset and turns it into an iterator over mini-batches - handling batching, shuffling, and (optionally) parallel workers for us:

::: {.xsmall}
```{r}
train_dl = dataloader(train_ds, batch_size = 64, shuffle = TRUE)

length(train_dl)
```
:::

. . .

::: {.xsmall}
```{r}
batch = train_dl$.iter()$.next()
str(batch, max.level = 1)
batch[[1]]$shape; batch[[2]]$shape
```
:::

::: {.aside}
`length(train_dl)` reports the number of mini-batches per epoch - here `ceiling(1437 / 64) = 23`.
:::


## Fitting with a dataloader

When `fit()` gets a dataloader instead of a `list(x, y)`, it iterates the dataloader directly - no `dataloader_options` needed:

::: {.xsmall}
```{r}
test_ds = tensor_dataset(X_test_t, y_test_t)
test_dl = dataloader(test_ds, batch_size = 64, shuffle = FALSE)

torch_manual_seed(1)
fitted_dl = mnist_fnn |>
  setup(
    loss = nn_cross_entropy_loss(),
    optimizer = optim_sgd,
    metrics = list(luz_metric_accuracy())
  ) |>
  set_hparams(hidden = 64, activation = nn_relu) |>
  set_opt_hparams(lr = 0.05) |>
  fit(
    train_dl,
    valid_data = test_dl,
    epochs = 30,
    verbose = FALSE
  )
```
:::

. . .

::: {.xsmall}
```{r}
get_metrics(fitted_dl) |> tail(4)
```
:::
